INSERT INTO main_app_journalentry VALUES(56,'2026-09-09','coach','Coach review (claude-opus-5)',replace('One session of evidence: 5 trades, all Opening Range Breakout, +$9.64 net on a $10k sim account (+0.10%). Three targets, two stops, PF 1.85 — statistically indistinguishable from noise at n=5, so nothing about today should drive parameter changes on its own. The real signal in this packet is cumulative: EMA Momentum has 19 live trades at 26% win rate, PF 0.50 and -$1.78 expectancy against a backtest that claimed 74% win rate and PF 2.88 — a drift large enough to treat the current EMA config as unvalidated. ORB is mildly positive over 26 trades (PF 1.25, last-20 expectancy $1.41) but has no stored baseline backtest to compare against. There is also an instrumentation puzzle: 4 of 9 signals were blocked citing ''max trades per day'' while only 5 of an allowed 12 trades were taken. I propose re-validating ORB exits and timing, a walk-forward re-fit of EMA Momentum off its pinned bounds, and a baseline search for the currently disabled VWAP Reversion before it is ever enabled.\n\n**Observations**\n- Today''s sample is far too small to conclude anything: 5 trades, all from one strategy, single session. A 60% win rate at n=5 has a confidence interval that spans coin-flip to excellent. recent_days is entirely empty (all null), so no multi-day trend can be assessed.\n- EMA Momentum shows severe live-vs-backtest drift: backtest 38 trades / 73.7% win / PF 2.88 / expectancy $5.04 versus live 19 trades / 26.3% win / PF 0.50 / expectancy -$1.78. Nineteen trades across only 3 sessions is still a small sample, but a win-rate gap of ~47 points in the direction of the fit is the classic signature of an over-optimized configuration rather than bad luck.\n- EMA Momentum''s live parameters sit at or near schema bounds: rsi_min 60.0 (schema max), min_relvol 2.0 (schema max), stop_atr_mult 2.0 (near max), rr 1.0 (min). A filter stack pinned at its extremes plus a 1:1 reward with a 2-ATR stop should produce a high win rate; the observed 26% contradicts the design intent, which points at the fit rather than the market.\n- ORB carries the whole book: 26 live trades over 4 sessions, PF 1.25, expectancy $0.91, last-20 expectancy $1.41. Positive but thin, and its baseline_backtest is an empty object — there is no reference distribution to detect drift against, which is a monitoring gap rather than a strategy problem.\n- Signal accounting looks inconsistent: 9 signals, 5 acted, 4 blocked with reason ''max trades per day'', while risk_limits.max_trades_per_day is 12 and only 5 trades were taken. Either the block reason is mislabeled (e.g. a per-symbol or per-strategy cap) or the counter is double-counting. Roughly 44% of signals were suppressed, so this matters for any honest read of strategy performance.\n- Cost drag is currently benign but not negligible: $0.8667 fees on $20.97 gross wins is ~4% of gross profit, and median slippage of 3.0 bps is exactly the configured slippage_bps constant, meaning the sim is reporting the assumption back, not measured execution quality. Expect the real number to differ once live.\n- Two ''restart'' risk events occurred, each preserving 0 positions. No harm done today, but restarts mid-session are a source of missed exits and untracked state if they ever coincide with an open position.\n\n**Hypotheses**\n- EMA Momentum''s parameters were fit to a narrow window and do not generalize; its filter stack (rsi_min 60, min_relvol 2.0) admits only late-stage extended moves, so entries arrive after the momentum is spent. A walk-forward re-fit with parameters pulled toward schema mid-range would test this.\n- ORB''s edge, if real, may be concentrated in exit geometry rather than entry selection: today''s 3-target/2-stop split at rr 2.0 with a 1.0-ATR stop suggests stops are wide enough to survive noise. Whether rr 2.0 is the right harvest point is untested and cheap to search.\n- The ''max trades per day'' blocks may be truncating ORB''s sample non-randomly — if blocks cluster in the later part of the entry window, measured performance reflects only early-session breakouts and the entry_window_minutes setting of 120 is effectively shorter than configured.\n- EMA''s negative expectancy and ORB''s positive one may partly reflect that ORB simply got more sessions and more trades; with 19 and 26 trades respectively, neither has crossed a threshold where per-strategy ranking is trustworthy.\n- Because both strategies show sub-$2 expectancy per trade at 0.5% risk sizing, any migration to crypto (25 bps taker per side, ~50 bps round trip) would likely erase the observed edge entirely — the current edge is small relative to plausible cost.\n\n**Risk flags**\n- Overfitting: EMA Momentum has multiple parameters pinned at schema bounds (rsi_min at max, min_relvol at max) and a backtest/live gap of 47 win-rate points. Treat the current config as invalid until re-validated; consider disabling it or reducing its size while the walk-forward runs.\n- Monitoring gap: ORB has no baseline_backtest recorded, so drift detection is impossible for the only strategy currently producing trades.\n- Instrumentation inconsistency: 4 signals blocked as ''max trades per day'' when 5 of 12 allowed trades were used. A risk limit is either mislabeled or firing incorrectly and is doing more work than the strategy — it suppressed 44% of signals today.\n- Slippage is a configured constant (3.0 bps), not measured. All PnL figures inherit that assumption; do not read slippage_median_bps as evidence of good execution.\n- Fee/cost burden: fees are ~4% of gross wins in stocks and tolerable, but the per-trade expectancy ($0.91-$1.93) is small enough that a move to crypto taker fees (25 bps) or any worsening of fills would flip these strategies negative.\n- Operational: two process restarts in one session. Zero positions were open by luck of timing, not by design.\n- Single-strategy concentration: 100% of today''s trades and effectively all positive live PnL come from ORB. Portfolio-level conclusions rest on one rule set.','\n',char(10)),'{"model": "claude-opus-5", "proposals": 4}','[{"title": "ORB exit geometry: stop width vs reward target", "strategy_key": "orb", "method": "grid", "param_grid": {"stop_atr_mult": [0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0], "rr": [1.5, 2.0, 2.5, 3.0]}, "rationale": "ORB is the only strategy with positive live expectancy, but its exit geometry has never been searched and no baseline backtest exists. A two-parameter sweep over stop width and reward multiple is the smallest experiment that both establishes a reference distribution and tests whether the current 1.0-ATR / 2.0-rr pairing is a plateau or a lucky point. Judge on stability across the neighbouring cells, not on the single best cell \u2014 with 26 live trades the incumbent has no claim to being optimal."}, {"title": "ORB entry timing: opening range length and entry window", "strategy_key": "orb", "method": "grid", "param_grid": {"range_minutes": [5, 15, 30], "entry_window_minutes": [60, 120, 180]}, "rationale": "Roughly 44% of today''s signals were blocked, and if those blocks cluster late in the session the effective entry window is shorter than the configured 120 minutes. Sweeping range length against window length shows whether the edge lives in the first hour or persists later, which also tells the operator whether the trade-count cap is truncating a real opportunity set or filtering junk."}, {"title": "EMA Momentum walk-forward re-fit off the schema bounds", "strategy_key": "ema_momentum", "method": "walk_forward", "param_grid": {"rsi_min": [45.0, 50.0, 55.0], "min_relvol": [1.0, 1.5, 2.0], "rr": [1.5, 2.0, 2.5]}, "rationale": "The live config has rsi_min and min_relvol pinned at their schema maxima and rr at its minimum, and it underperforms its own backtest by 47 win-rate points. Walk-forward is the right method here because the failure mode is generalization, not parameter level: only an out-of-sample-by-construction search can show whether any setting survives. Values deliberately span the mid-range to test whether relaxed filters and a wider target restore the designed behaviour. If no fold shows positive out-of-sample expectancy, the honest conclusion is to keep the strategy disabled rather than re-tune it again."}, {"title": "VWAP Reversion baseline before any enablement", "strategy_key": "vwap_reversion", "method": "grid", "param_grid": {"entry_z": [1.5, 2.0, 2.5], "lookback": [20, 30, 40, 60], "max_bars_held": [12, 24, 36]}, "rationale": "VWAP Reversion is disabled with zero trades and no baseline_backtest, so there is nothing to drift against and no basis for turning it on. This grid establishes a first reference over the three parameters that define the mean-reversion thesis: how far from VWAP to enter, how the sigma is estimated, and how long to wait for reversion. Treat the output as a baseline to be beaten, not a green light \u2014 a strategy with no live sample should not be enabled on backtest evidence alone, especially given how badly EMA Momentum''s backtest translated."}]','2026-09-09 17:04:08.564786',1);
INSERT INTO main_app_journalentry VALUES(57,'2026-09-09','coach','Coach review (claude-opus-5)',replace('One session of evidence (5 trades, all ORB, +$9.64 net) with no usable prior-day history — far too small to judge anything about today. The one substantive signal in this packet is the EMA Momentum drift: a backtest showing 73.7% win rate and PF 2.88 over 38 trades versus live 19 trades at 26.3% win rate, PF 0.50 and -$1.78 expectancy. Its live parameters also sit pinned at schema bounds (rsi_min 60 = max, min_relvol 2.0 = max, rr 1.0 = min), which is the classic fingerprint of a fitted configuration rather than a robust one. Secondary issue: half of today''s signals were rejected by a ''max trades per day'' cap of 5 while the documented risk limit is 12 — a limit, not the strategy, decided most of today''s activity. Proposals below re-search EMA Momentum away from its bounds, establish an honest ORB parameter surface (it has no baseline backtest at all), and build a first baseline for the disabled VWAP Reversion before it is ever enabled.\n\n**Observations**\n- Today is a single session of 5 closed trades, all from ORB, 3 targets and 2 stops. Net +$9.64 on ~$9,990 equity (+0.10%). At n=5 this is indistinguishable from noise; win rate 60% and PF 1.85 carry no information.\n- recent_days contains 10 entries that are entirely null, so there is no day-over-day trend, no drift trajectory, and no way to tell whether today was typical. All conclusions here rest on the cumulative live_stats and the one EMA backtest.\n- EMA Momentum shows the largest and best-supported gap in the packet: baseline backtest 38 trades / 73.7% win / PF 2.88 / expectancy +$5.04 versus live 19 trades / 26.3% win / PF 0.50 / expectancy -$1.78. Same direction on both halves of the ratio (fewer wins AND worse win/loss size), across 3 sessions. 19 trades is still thin, but a PF collapse from 2.88 to 0.50 is a large effect, not a rounding error.\n- EMA Momentum''s live params sit on schema boundaries: rsi_min 60.0 (max allowed), min_relvol 2.0 (max allowed), rr 1.0 (min allowed), stop_atr_mult 2.0 (near max). Boundary-pinned parameters are usually the residue of a search that ran out of room rather than a genuine optimum.\n- EMA Momentum''s payoff geometry is unfavourable on its face: stop at 2.0 ATR with rr 1.0 means it risks two ATRs to make two ATRs and therefore needs well above 50% win rate net of costs to break even. Live win rate is 26.3%.\n- ORB has no baseline_backtest at all (empty object), so its live PF of 1.25 over 26 trades cannot be compared to any expectation. Its last_20 expectancy (+$1.41) is above its all-time expectancy (+$0.91), which is mildly encouraging and nothing more at this sample size.\n- Blocked signals: 10 signals, 5 acted, 5 rejected by ''max trades per day'' reported as 5 — while risk_limits.max_trades_per_day is 12. Either an undocumented per-strategy/per-session cap is binding or the limits are inconsistent. Half of today''s opportunity set was decided by a governor rather than by strategy logic.\n- Cost burden is real but not alarming for stocks: $0.8667 of fees against $9.64 net (about 9% of net, ~4% of gross win) and median slippage 3.0 bps. Note that 3.0 bps is exactly the configured slippage_bps for every strategy, so slippage here is a simulator assumption being echoed back, not measured evidence.\n- Two ''restart'' risk events, both preserving 0 positions. Operationally benign this session, but a restart while positions are open is an untested path.\n- VWAP Reversion is disabled with 0 trades and no backtest. It is currently an unknown, not a spare tyre.\n\n**Hypotheses**\n- The EMA Momentum backtest is overfit and/or the live params were tuned to it. rsi_min at 60 plus min_relvol at 2.0 select a narrow, high-momentum regime; if that regime is rare or has shifted, live entries fall into a different distribution than the 38 backtest trades. Testable by re-searching those two parameters away from their bounds.\n- Independently of selection, rr 1.0 with a 2.0 ATR stop may simply be an unprofitable payoff structure once fees and slippage are paid. Testable by sweeping rr and stop_atr_mult.\n- The backtest/live gap may be partly execution and partly sample: 19 trades over 3 sessions could include a bad cluster. If a wider parameter search still shows negative expectancy across most of the surface, the strategy is broken, not unlucky — that distinction is what the sweep is for.\n- ORB''s mildly positive live edge may be coming from the low min_relvol (0.5, below the 1.0 default) admitting more breakouts, or it may be a symbol-selection artefact. Unknown without a baseline surface.\n- The 5-trade cap may be truncating ORB''s session before its better setups appear; equally it may be protecting the book. Cannot be resolved from statistics — it needs the operator to reconcile the configured limit first.\n\n**Risk flags**\n- OVERFITTING: EMA Momentum has three parameters pinned to schema bounds and a backtest PF (2.88) nearly 6x its live PF (0.50). Treat the baseline backtest as unreliable until reproduced out-of-sample.\n- LIMIT DOING THE STRATEGY''S WORK: 5 of 10 signals blocked by a ''max trades per day'' value of 5 that contradicts the documented limit of 12. Reconcile the configuration before drawing any conclusion from today''s per-strategy stats — the sample is censored.\n- SAMPLE TOO SMALL: today n=5; EMA live n=19 over 3 sessions; ORB live n=26 over 4 sessions; recent_days entirely empty. Do not promote, disable, or size up anything on this evidence.\n- NO BASELINE: ORB and VWAP Reversion have empty baseline_backtest objects. Drift detection is impossible for the strategy currently doing all the trading.\n- EMA Momentum is live and enabled while running negative expectancy (-$1.78/trade) and a losing PF. Consider parking it in research until a sweep identifies a defensible region, rather than continuing to pay for discovery with the book.\n- MEASUREMENT ARTEFACT: median slippage is identical (3.0 bps) across all strategies and equals the configured slippage_bps. Slippage is currently modelled, not observed; real-money execution will differ and the crypto path would additionally pay 25 bps taker per side, which would swamp the small per-trade edges seen here.\n- SINGLE-STRATEGY CONCENTRATION: 100% of today''s trades and P&L came from ORB across 5 correlated large-cap tech/index names. Diversification is nominal.','\n',char(10)),'{"model": "claude-opus-5", "proposals": 4}','[{"title": "EMA Momentum: unpin the payoff geometry (rr, stop, RSI floor)", "strategy_key": "ema_momentum", "method": "grid", "param_grid": {"rr": [1.5, 2.0, 2.5, 3.0], "stop_atr_mult": [1.0, 1.5, 2.0], "rsi_min": [45.0, 50.0, 55.0]}, "rationale": "Live rr is at its schema minimum (1.0) with a wide 2.0 ATR stop, requiring a high win rate the strategy is not delivering (26.3%). rsi_min is at its maximum (60). This sweep moves all three off the boundary and asks whether any region of the surface produces positive expectancy after the 3 bps slippage and fees. Read the result as a map, not a winner: if most cells are negative, the honest conclusion is to retire the config rather than pick the best cell."}, {"title": "EMA Momentum: does the relative-volume/EMA filter actually earn its selectivity?", "strategy_key": "ema_momentum", "method": "walk_forward", "param_grid": {"min_relvol": [0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0], "fast": [7, 9, 11], "slow": [19, 23, 27]}, "rationale": "min_relvol sits at the schema maximum (2.0), the tightest possible entry filter, and fast/slow (11/27) both sit above their defaults. Walk-forward is deliberate here: the suspicion is that the 38-trade backtest was fitted, so out-of-sample folds are the only way to tell a real filter from a curve-fit one. Include the default 1.0 and the looser 0.5 so the current setting has to beat a plainer alternative."}, {"title": "ORB: build the missing baseline surface (rr, stop, relvol)", "strategy_key": "orb", "method": "walk_forward", "param_grid": {"rr": [1.5, 2.0, 2.5, 3.0], "stop_atr_mult": [0.5, 1.0, 1.5], "min_relvol": [0.5, 1.0, 1.5]}, "rationale": "ORB is producing all current live activity (26 trades, PF 1.25) with no baseline_backtest to compare against, so drift cannot be detected at all. This establishes an out-of-sample expectation and checks whether the current below-default min_relvol of 0.5 is a genuine edge or just extra trade count. A flat, broad plateau across rr and stop would be reassuring; a single sharp peak would mean the surface is noise at this sample size."}, {"title": "ORB: opening-range length and how long the entry window stays open", "strategy_key": "orb", "method": "grid", "param_grid": {"range_minutes": [5, 15, 30], "entry_window_minutes": [60, 120, 180]}, "rationale": "These two structural timing parameters have never been varied and interact directly: a 5-minute range with a 180-minute window is a very different strategy from a 30-minute range with a 60-minute window. Only two parameters so the grid stays small and the result is interpretable. Run this after the trade-cap inconsistency is fixed, since a cap of 5 truncates later-session entries and would bias any comparison of longer entry windows."}]','2026-09-09 20:01:21.588586',1);
INSERT INTO main_app_journalentry VALUES(58,'2026-09-09','coach','Coach review (claude-opus-5)',replace('Burst is the only strategy that has traded, and it has lost money in every one of the five sessions on record (81 trades, roughly -$1,462, account down ~25% from $10k). Today''s 0/12 is a small sample on its own, but stacked on 9/02-9/07 it is no longer noise: the configuration as shipped does not have an edge. The clearest mechanical explanation is trade geometry versus cost. At 25 bps taker each way plus ~3 bps median slippage, a round trip costs ~0.62%; with stop_pct 1.0 and target_pct 1.5 the realized reward:risk is roughly 0.88%:1.62% (~0.54), which needs a ~65% hit rate to break even against an observed hit rate near 14%. Today produced 10 stops, 2 time exits and zero targets, which is what that geometry predicts. Separately, live burst runs with min_relvol 0.0 against a schema default of 1.5, so there is no quality filter on the trigger bar, and risk limits are absorbing most of the flow (56 signals, 16 acted, 24 blocks on the directional exposure cap, 16 on max open positions, and the 12-trade daily cap hit exactly). Until a search shows a target that clears cost by a wide margin, my recommendation is to keep burst at sim size or re-quarantine it rather than tune it while live.\n\n**Observations**\n- Burst is negative in all five recorded sessions (-131.21, -631.45, -385.73, -43.17, -268.97). Cumulative ~81 trades, ~11 wins (~14% win rate), profit factor between 0.05 and 0.57 every day. That is enough evidence to reject the current parameter set, even though today''s 12-trade 0% win rate alone would not be.\n- Cost burden is the dominant term: today''s fees were $85.93 against a $268.97 net loss (32%). A 25 bps taker round trip plus ~3 bps slippage per side is ~0.62%, so the 1.5% target nets ~0.88% while the 1.0% stop costs ~1.62% — an effective RR of ~0.54, not the 1.5 the parameters imply.\n- Exit mix confirms the geometry problem, not a signal-direction problem: 10 stops / 2 time / 0 targets today, and across 9/02-9/04 time exits dominated (8 of 11, 16 of 22). Trades are either stopped by noise or expire flat, rarely reaching target.\n- Live burst params show min_relvol 0.0 while the schema default is 1.5 — the relative-volume quality filter is effectively off, so any 0.6% five-bar move qualifies.\n- Risk limits, not the strategy, are shaping the day: only 16 of 56 signals were acted on, with 24 blocks from the directional exposure cap and 16 from max open positions (cap 4), and the 12-trade daily limit was reached. With a long-only all-crypto basket, correlated signals arrive together and the caps decide which ones fill — a quasi-random selection on top of a negative-expectancy signal.\n- The quarantine-lift note says burst was moved to 15Min-capable observation reasoning, and ema_momentum was enabled ''to observe on 15Min bars'', but risk_limits.timeframe is 5Min. Worth confirming the bar size actually in use before attributing results to either regime.\n- ema_momentum has zero trades and no baseline backtest recorded, so nothing can be said about it yet.\n\n**Hypotheses**\n- Primary: the loss is structural cost/geometry, not signal quality. A 1.0% stop on high-volatility degen pairs is inside normal bar noise, so stops resolve first, and the 1.5% target is only ~2.4x the round-trip cost. Widening the target-to-cost ratio and the stop-to-noise ratio should be tested before touching entry logic.\n- Secondary: with min_relvol at 0.0 and min_move_pct at 0.6, the trigger fires on low-conviction drift rather than genuine bursts, inflating trade count into the daily cap and diluting whatever edge exists in real volume expansion.\n- Tertiary: churn. cooldown_bars 10 on a 5Min timeframe allows re-entry on the same symbol within ~50 minutes; BONK saw 3 trades and PEPE/DOGE/ADA 2 each today, consistent with repeated re-entry into the same failing move and paying the round trip each time.\n- Possible but unsupported: exposure caps may be systematically filtering toward the worst signals (first-arriving rather than best-scoring). Cannot be tested with the fields given — would need blocked-signal outcomes tracked counterfactually.\n\n**Risk flags**\n- Fee/slippage burden is the headline risk: at ~0.62% round trip, any target below ~2% on crypto is fighting a large fixed drag. Do not accept a search result whose winning cell relies on a sub-2% target.\n- Risk limits are doing more work than the strategy. Max open positions (4) and the directional exposure cap blocked 40 of 56 signals; the daily trade cap bound exactly at 12. Improvements observed after tuning could be caps truncating losses rather than edge.\n- Drawdown: account equity $7,522 versus $10,000 starting cash (-24.8%), with today alone -2.7% on the strategy''s own max_drawdown_pct and a 2.0% max daily loss limit. The strategy is generating losses at roughly the pace the daily loss limit is designed to stop.\n- Overfitting exposure is high: no baseline_backtest is recorded for either strategy, and only ~81 live burst trades exist. Any parameter cell that looks good on this sample is likely noise-fitted. Require out-of-sample or walk-forward confirmation and a minimum trade count per cell before promoting anything.\n- Zero wins in 12 trades is attention-getting but is not by itself statistically decisive; treat it as consistent with the prior four sessions rather than as new information.\n- ema_momentum is enabled with trade_short false in a long-only, all-crypto basket — it will inherit the same correlated-exposure and cap-contention problems burst has, before it produces any evidence.','\n',char(10)),'{"model": "claude-opus-5", "proposals": 3}','[{"title": "Burst: cost-aware stop/target geometry", "strategy_key": "burst", "method": "grid", "param_grid": {"stop_pct": [1.2, 1.8, 2.4], "target_pct": [2.5, 3.5, 4.5], "min_relvol": [1.5, 2.0]}, "rationale": "Directly tests the primary hypothesis. Every cell has a target of at least 4x the ~0.62% round-trip cost and a stop wide enough to sit outside single-bar noise, so a win actually pays for itself. min_relvol is restored to at least the schema default to stop firing on low-conviction drift. Read this on target-vs-stop exit counts and expectancy net of fees, not on win rate alone \u2014 and discard any cell with fewer than ~30 trades."}, {"title": "Burst: selectivity and churn control", "strategy_key": "burst", "method": "random", "param_grid": {"min_move_pct": [0.8, 1.2, 1.6], "lookback": [5, 9, 13], "cooldown_bars": [15, 30, 45]}, "rationale": "Tests the over-trading hypotheses. Raising the move threshold and lengthening the lookback should demand a real burst rather than drift; longer cooldowns should stop repeated paid re-entries into the same failing move (BONK 3x, PEPE/DOGE/ADA 2x today). Success criterion is fewer trades with better expectancy and fewer blocks on the exposure cap \u2014 if trade count falls but expectancy stays negative, the entry signal itself has no edge and burst should go back to quarantine."}, {"title": "EMA momentum: establish a walk-forward baseline before it risks capital", "strategy_key": "ema_momentum", "method": "walk_forward", "param_grid": {"rr": [2.0, 3.0, 4.0], "stop_atr_mult": [1.0, 1.5, 2.0], "min_relvol": [1.0, 1.5]}, "rationale": "This strategy has zero trades and no recorded baseline, so it should be characterized out-of-sample rather than observed live alongside a losing strategy competing for the same four position slots. Walk-forward is the right method precisely because the burst experience shows in-sample-looking numbers here would not be trustworthy. The rr and stop_atr_mult sweep answers the same cost question \u2014 with a 1.5 ATR stop, rr must be high enough that the target clears 0.62% round trip on these symbols."}]','2026-09-10 04:01:12.809850',7);
INSERT INTO main_app_journalentry VALUES(59,'2026-09-09','coach','Coach review (claude-opus-5)',replace('One live session (2026-09-09) with 12 closed trades is the entire evidence base — the prior day had zero trades and the rest of the window is empty, so nothing here is statistically conclusive. The session ended +$87.62 (PF 1.34) but that came entirely from vwap_reversion (+$177.51 on 6 trades) while ema_momentum lost $89.89 on 6 trades; cumulative live stats echo the split (vwap 20 trades PF 1.33, ema 10 trades PF 0.35, 20% win rate). The more informative signals are structural, not P&L: 19 of 35 signals were blocked, the daily trade cap was reached exactly, and fees ($59.14) consumed roughly 40% of gross wins against an expectancy of $7.30/trade. Proposals below are exploratory parameter searches to widen the ema_momentum evidence and to stress-test whether vwap_reversion''s edge is parameter-stable, not endorsements of either strategy.\n\n**Observations**\n- Sample is far too small for any performance conclusion: 12 closed trades in one session, 2 sessions total per strategy, and no backtest baselines recorded for either strategy. A 41.7% win rate and PF 1.34 over 12 trades is indistinguishable from noise.\n- Blocked signals (19) outnumbered acted signals (16). The largest categories are ''remaining capacity would create an undersized position'' (8) and ''directional exposure cap reached'' (7) — i.e. sizing and exposure plumbing, not strategy logic, decided most of the day''s participation.\n- Trades (12) equals max_trades_per_day (12), so the day was truncated by the cap. Any per-day P&L or win-rate figure is therefore a censored sample, and the last signals of the session were never given a chance.\n- Fee burden is material at this size: $59.14 of fees on 12 trades (~$4.93/trade) versus $7.30 expectancy per trade. Roughly 40% of gross winnings went to costs, so any parameter change that increases trade count without increasing edge per trade will likely turn the session negative.\n- Realized slippage was 0.3 bps median while the configured assumption is 3.0 bps — a 10x conservative gap. Backtests using the configured value will understate forex results; the mismatch should be reconciled before comparing live to backtest drift.\n- Exit mix is stop-heavy: 6 stops vs 3 targets, plus 2 signal exits and 1 time exit. Consistent with stops being hit before targets, but 12 observations cannot distinguish ''stops too tight'' from an ordinary losing streak.\n- allow_short is false while a directional exposure cap is in force, so every position is long and the cap binds quickly. Two positions were still open at session end and a restart preserved one position — carried risk is not reflected in the closed-trade stats.\n\n**Hypotheses**\n- ema_momentum''s entry filters may be admitting low-quality continuation signals in forex (min_relvol 1.0 is weakly meaningful on FX where volume is broker-specific), producing the 20% win rate; a stricter RSI band or wider stop could change the stop/target hit ratio. Untested — 10 trades.\n- Because rr is 2.0 with stop_atr_mult 1.5, ema_momentum needs ~34% win rate just to break even before fees; at ~4.9 of fees per trade the break-even is higher still. The parameter pair, not the signal, may be the binding problem.\n- vwap_reversion''s positive result could be concentrated in one symbol/trade (best trade NZD/USD +$173.44 on a day NZD/USD netted only +$38.82 across 6 trades), suggesting the strategy total is one large winner offset by several small losers — a fragile shape worth verifying with walk-forward.\n- The ''undersized position'' blocks suggest risk_per_trade_pct (0.5%) interacts with max_open_positions (4) such that the 3rd/4th slot cannot be filled at meaningful size. If so, the effective strategy is ''first two signals of the day'' rather than the documented rule set.\n\n**Risk flags**\n- Daily trade cap (12) was hit exactly — the risk limit, not the strategy, determined the end of the session. Treat today''s P&L as a censored sample.\n- Fees at ~40% of gross wins; expectancy per trade ($7.30) is only ~1.5x average fee per trade. Fee/slippage burden is the dominant cost line at current position sizing.\n- Sizing and exposure blocks rejected 15 of 35 signals; capacity plumbing is doing more work than the entry logic. Investigate before attributing results to parameters.\n- Configured slippage (3.0 bps) is 10x realized median (0.3 bps) — backtest-to-live comparisons will be biased and drift detection unreliable until reconciled.\n- Both strategies were enabled today (qualification events same-day). Zero out-of-sample history exists; any parameter tuned on this single session is at high risk of overfitting to 12 trades.\n- Two positions open and a restart-preserved position mean unrealized risk is excluded from the reported PF/expectancy.','\n',char(10)),'{"model": "claude-opus-5", "proposals": 4}','[{"title": "ema_momentum: stop/target geometry and RSI floor", "strategy_key": "ema_momentum", "method": "grid", "param_grid": {"stop_atr_mult": [1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 2.5], "rr": [1.5, 2.0, 3.0], "rsi_min": [45.0, 50.0, 55.0]}, "rationale": "Six stops vs three targets across the session, and a 20% live win rate on 10 trades, point at stop/target geometry as the first thing to characterize. Widening the stop lowers the required win rate; lowering rr does the same at the cost of average win size. rsi_min is included because it directly gates how extended the entry is. The purpose is to map the win-rate/payoff surface over a longer backtest window, not to pick today''s best cell \u2014 with 10 live trades no cell can be validated."}, {"title": "ema_momentum: EMA pair sensitivity and volume gate", "strategy_key": "ema_momentum", "method": "grid", "param_grid": {"fast": [5, 9, 13], "slow": [15, 21, 29], "min_relvol": [0.0, 0.5, 1.0, 1.5]}, "rationale": "Tests whether the trend definition itself is the problem rather than the exits. Including min_relvol at 0.0 checks whether the relative-volume gate adds anything in forex, where volume is broker-synthetic; if results are flat across min_relvol, the filter is decoration and should be documented as such. Watch trade counts closely \u2014 looser settings that raise frequency will be penalized by the ~25 bps-equivalent cost drag seen today."}, {"title": "vwap_reversion: entry threshold and hold window", "strategy_key": "vwap_reversion", "method": "grid", "param_grid": {"entry_z": [1.5, 2.0, 2.5, 3.0], "lookback": [20, 30, 40], "max_bars_held": [12, 24, 36]}, "rationale": "The only strategy with a positive live result, but on 20 trades over 2 sessions and with one trade (+$173) plausibly carrying the total. This search characterizes how much of the result depends on the specific z-threshold and hold window. A sharp peak at entry_z 2.0 with poor neighbours would be evidence of luck rather than edge; a broad plateau would be mildly reassuring. Higher entry_z reduces trade count, which also reduces the fee drag that ate 40% of gross wins today."}, {"title": "vwap_reversion: walk-forward stability of threshold and stop", "strategy_key": "vwap_reversion", "method": "walk_forward", "param_grid": {"entry_z": [1.5, 2.0, 2.5], "stop_atr_mult": [1.0, 1.5, 2.0], "min_bar_pos": [3, 6, 9]}, "rationale": "Explicit out-of-sample check on the only candidate showing an edge, before anyone proposes promoting it past Sprout. Walk-forward is chosen so parameters are selected in-sample and scored out-of-sample; if out-of-sample expectancy collapses versus in-sample, the strategy stays in fake-money observation. min_bar_pos is included because session-open behaviour is a common source of look-alike edge that does not survive reshuffled windows."}]','2026-09-10 04:01:14.667847',10);
